{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791058029,"nonce":"4yFNL30YrnKTz2oenN1xuMN7I7Y=","title":"Host heap seam: a host's allocator and collector behind the runtime's allocation entry points (M1)","message":"Caribou runs Python and Lua under its own heap and conservative collector, and today has to run them with Collector::None (the runtime's MarkSweep assumes one mutator thread), so they leak. M1: a versioned #[repr(C)] table (version, size, context, alloc(size, kind) zeroed 16-aligned, free, realloc, owns, add_root_range, add_root_spans(start, len, reader), remove_root_range, thread_enter(stack_top), thread_leave) installed once; zyntax_alloc/free/realloc delegate to it (blocks it does not own keep their own path); the runtime's collector stays off; roots registered through collector::add_root_range* go to the host, span readers through a C trampoline; threads the runtime starts announce themselves; what the runtime names from Rust-side tables (parked futures, completions, handler/performer pairs, promises' state machines, handler instances, fiber environments and error payloads) is kept by a global hold set registered as a root, and closure environments are Held allocations. Fiber stacks are registered whole while the fiber lives. Zyntax never names the host.\n\nLater stages, not here: M2 descriptor word per object (TypeMeta onto the host's type descriptors), M3 precise pointer maps.","files":null}]}